workedExamples.question_text:
For this connected planar drawing, state what $v$, $e$ and $f$ count, find their values and verify Euler's formula.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (E) at (1.5,1.2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(E)--(C);
\draw (B)--(E)--(D);
\foreach \p in {A,B,C,D,E}{\fill[black] (\p) circle (2pt);}
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$} (B) node[below right] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$} (D) node[above left] {$D$} (E) node[above,yshift=2pt] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$v$ counts vertices, $e$ counts edges and $f$ counts faces, including the outer region. The central dot is a vertex, so each corner-to-centre segment is a separate edge.
There are $5$ vertices, $8$ edges and $5$ faces: four enclosed regions and the outer region.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (E) at (1.5,1.2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(E)--(C);
\draw (B)--(E)--(D);
\foreach \p in {A,B,C,D,E}{\fill[black] (\p) circle (2pt);}
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$} (B) node[below right] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$} (D) node[above left] {$D$} (E) node[above,yshift=2pt] {$E$};
\path (1.5,0.4) node {$F_1$} (2.5,1.2) node {$F_2$} (1.5,2) node {$F_3$} (0.5,1.2) node {$F_4$} (4.1,1.2) node {$F_5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}v-e+f\\&=5-8+5\\&=2\end{aligned}$
Euler's formula holds.

workedExamples.question_text:
This connected planar drawing has a loop at $D$ and two edges joining $A$ and $B$. Count its vertices and edges, use Euler's formula to find its faces, then verify the result by counting regions.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2.2);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A) .. controls (0.8,-1.2) and (2.2,-1.2) .. (B);
\draw (D) .. controls (-1.5,3.5) and (1.5,3.5) .. (D);
\foreach \p in {A,B,C,D}{\fill[black] (\p) circle (2pt);}
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[right] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$} (D) node[left] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The loop counts as one edge. Count both edges joining $A$ and $B$ separately. Thus $v=4$ and $e=6$.
$\begin{aligned}v-e+f&=2\\4-6+f&=2\\f&=2-4+6\\&=4\end{aligned}$
The four faces are the rectangle's interior, the region between the two edges $AB$, the loop's interior and the outer region.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2.2);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A) .. controls (0.8,-1.2) and (2.2,-1.2) .. (B);
\draw (D) .. controls (-1.5,3.5) and (1.5,3.5) .. (D);
\foreach \p in {A,B,C,D}{\fill[black] (\p) circle (2pt);}
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[right] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$} (D) node[left] {$D$};
\path (1.5,1.1) node {$F_1$} (1.5,-0.45) node {$F_2$} (0,2.8) node {$F_3$} (4.1,1.1) node {$F_4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}v-e+f\\&=4-6+4\\&=2\end{aligned}$

(a)
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}v-e+f\\&=7-11+5\\&=1\end{aligned}$
These counts are impossible: a connected planar drawing must give $2$.
(b)
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}v-e+f\\&=7-11+6\\&=2\end{aligned}$
Satisfying the formula alone does not establish existence. Draw a convex heptagon $ABCDEFG$ and add diagonals $AC$, $AD$, $AE$ and $AF$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1,3.4);
\coordinate (C) at (2.8,3.4);
\coordinate (D) at (4,2.2);
\coordinate (E) at (3.5,0.5);
\coordinate (F) at (1.8,-0.2);
\coordinate (G) at (0.2,0.5);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--(E)--(F)--(G)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(C) (A)--(D) (A)--(E) (A)--(F);
\foreach \p in {A,B,C,D,E,F,G}{\fill[black] (\p) circle (2pt);}
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[above left] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$} (D) node[right] {$D$} (E) node[below right] {$E$} (F) node[below] {$F$} (G) node[below left] {$G$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The diagonals meet only at $A$. This connected drawing has no crossings, $7$ vertices, $7+4=11$ edges and five enclosed faces plus the outer face. Therefore, the counts in (b) are possible.

